    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d711">

                <pb facs="0812" n="700" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg700"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.9318/187: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-18T19:00:00-04:00">June 18, 1935—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>170. Your 279, June 15, 2 p.m.<anchor n="24" xml:id="d711-f1"/> The Department in
                    reply to inquiries <pb facs="0813" n="701" xml:id="pg701"/> from the press has
                    stated that there has been no consideration nor discussion here in regard to the
                    removal of American troops from Peiping and Tientsin.</p>
                <p>There has been no press inquiry in regard to the removal of the Legation.<anchor
                        n="25" xml:id="d711-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d711-f1" n="24" xml:id="d711-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d711-f2" n="25" xml:id="d711-f2-content">
                <p>See section entitled, “Raising of the American Legation in China to the Status of
                    Embassy and Abolition of the Consulate General at Nanking,” <ref target="#pg508"
                        >pp. 508</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
